def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'ns_dgmr','label':'Nova Scotia: Halifax, Cape Breton, the Valley and the Northumberland shore','added':'2026-11-23 19:36'}
PLACES=[
N('halifax-citadel','Halifax Citadel','Halifax','ns','fort','standing',44.6475,-63.58028,'exact','open','walk','forts','1794–1800',
  'Four forts have stood on Citadel Hill since Halifax was founded in 1749; the one you see is the third, built between 1794 and 1800 and named Fort George, then restored to its Victorian appearance. It guarded Halifax harbour and the Royal Navy dockyard for over a century and was never attacked.',
  'A star-shaped stone and earth fort on the hill above downtown, with its ramparts, ditch, a visitor centre, period rooms and exhibits. In the cold months only the parade square is open; the buildings and the top of the walls are closed.',
  '',
  'Run by Parks Canada; fees in the open season. The grounds and parade square are open year-round except December 25. Street and mapped lots below the hill; parking not checked on the ground.',
  [L('Parks Canada: hours of operation','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/ns/halifax/visit/heures-hours'),W('Citadel Hill (Fort George)')],
  brick='?',hours='Grounds daily except Dec 25. Nov to early May: parade square only, buildings and ramparts closed. In season about 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (weekends 9 to 4 some weeks)'),
N('fortress-of-louisbourg','Fortress of Louisbourg','Louisbourg','ns','fort','ruins',45.89207,-59.98541,'site','open','drive','forts','1720–1760',
  'France built Louisbourg on Cape Breton Island as a naval base and fishing port; its walls went up mainly between 1720 and 1740, and by the mid-1740s it was one of the most expensive fortifications in North America. It fell to the British in 1758, and its stone was later carried off to Halifax and Sydney. From 1961 into the 1970s about a fifth of the town and two bastions were rebuilt on the old foundations. The site became a national historic site in 1920.',
  'The reconstructed town of stone and timber buildings behind the Dauphin Gate and the King\'s Bastion, with costumed staff in summer, and beyond it the unrebuilt fortifications and ruins along the shore and on Rochfort Point. In the low season the site is open as a green space for self-guided walks.',
  'Reconstruction on a French fortress that stood from 1720.',
  'Run by Parks Canada; admission in season. Open Monday to Friday from November 1, 2026 to May 17, 2027 as a green space; seven days a week in the shoulder and peak seasons. The pin is the middle of the rebuilt town; the visitor reception and parking are by the site entrance on the road in (not checked on the ground).',
  [L('Parks Canada: hours of operation','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/ns/louisbourg/visit/heures-hours'),W('Fortress of Louisbourg')],
  brick='no',hours='Nov 1 2026 to May 17 2027: Monday to Friday, grounds only. May 18 to Sept 11: daily. Sept 12 to Oct 31: daily'),
N('prince-of-wales-tower-halifax','Prince of Wales Tower','Halifax','ns','fort','standing',44.62157,-63.56968,'exact','view','walk','forts','1796–',
  'Built in 1796 by Captain James Straton as a redoubt and powder magazine, when the Duke of Kent was strengthening the batteries round Halifax harbour. It is a squat round stone tower, almost three times as wide as it is high, in the style of the Martello towers built elsewhere by the British army. Of five such towers in Halifax it is the last one standing.',
  'A low round stone tower in the woods of Point Pleasant Park, near the harbour entrance. Whether the inside can be visited was not checked.',
  '',
  'In Point Pleasant Park, a city park open to walkers (ownership not checked). The nearest mapped parking is along Point Pleasant Drive, 600 m or more away; I did not check it on the ground. Interior access not checked.',
  [W('Prince of Wales Tower')],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-edward-blockhouse-windsor','Fort Edward Blockhouse','Windsor','ns','fort','standing',44.99651,-64.13516,'exact','view','walk','forts','1750–',
  'Major Charles Lawrence built Fort Edward in 1750 with wooden barracks, a storehouse and a blockhouse inside a palisaded square with four bastions. Wikipedia says the Acadians were made to destroy their church on the spot to make room for it. The blockhouse is all that is left of the fort and is described as the oldest blockhouse still standing in North America. It was a training depot in the First World War, and David Ben-Gurion drilled here in 1918.',
  'A wooden blockhouse on a grassy rise, with earth ramparts and ditches of the old fort traceable around it and a cairn. The Register says the blockhouse has been restored and opened for visits; no opening hours were found.',
  'The oldest blockhouse in North America, on the site of a destroyed Acadian church.',
  'National historic site at Fort Edward Street, Windsor; the Register says the blockhouse is open for visits, but ownership and hours were not checked. Street parking by the fort.',
  [HP('Fort Edward National Historic Site of Canada',7419),W('Fort Edward (Nova Scotia)')],
  brick='no'),
N('clifton-haliburton-house-windsor','Clifton (Haliburton House)','Windsor','ns','landmark','standing',44.98896,-64.14248,'exact','open','drive','','1830s–',
  'Built in the 1830s for Thomas Chandler Haliburton, the Windsor-born judge and writer who created Sam Slick. He sold the house and its contents when he left for England in 1856. The province bought it in 1939 and opened it as a museum in 1940, and it is now part of the Nova Scotia Museum.',
  'A large house furnished with period pieces and some of Haliburton\'s own things, including his writing desk, on a 34-acre park of lawns, ponds, woods and orchards. The park is open all day all year; the house is open on a summer schedule.',
  '',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum; fee for the house. The park is free and open all year. Parking not checked; Lakeview Drive runs beside the park. Hours below are as the museum\'s pages were quoted in search results.',
  [L('Nova Scotia Museum: Clifton Museum Park','https://clifton.novascotia.ca/visit'),W('Clifton Museum Park')],
  brick='?',hours='Early June to early October, Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (park open daily all year)'),
N('uniacke-house-mount-uniacke','Uniacke Estate House','Mount Uniacke','ns','landmark','standing',44.90156,-63.84442,'exact','open','drive','','c.1813–',
  'The country house of Richard John Uniacke, Nova Scotia\'s Attorney-General during the War of 1812, built about 1813. It stayed in the Uniacke family with few changes until the province bought it in 1949, and it opened as a museum on June 2, 1952.',
  'A big country house with a restored interior and a tea room, a barn and carriage house that can only be looked into from the doorways, and a network of trails that includes a 2.5 km piece of the old stagecoach road from Halifax to Windsor.',
  '',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum; fee for the house. The trails are open all year. Parking is by the house in the museum season; a mapped lot on Hothouse Hill Loop is 275 m west. Hours as quoted from the museum\'s page in search results; confirm before going.',
  [L('Uniacke Estate Museum Park: visit','https://uniacke.novascotia.ca/visit'),W('Uniacke Estate Museum Park')],
  brick='?',hours='June 1 to Sept 30, Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (trails open all year)'),
N('grand-pre-national-historic-site','Grand-Pré National Historic Site','Grand-Pré','ns','town','standing',45.10991,-64.31231,'site','open','drive','','1682–1755; 1922–',
  'From 1682 to 1755 Grand-Pré was the centre of Acadian settlement on the Minas Basin, and in 1755 it was the headquarters of the deportation of more than six thousand Acadians. A memorial park was begun here in 1907 and laid out in 1922 for the Dominion Atlantic Railway to the design of Percy Nobbs. An Acadian society built the stone memorial church, designed by René-Arthur Fréchet, to recall the original church of Saint-Charles, and Philippe Hébert made the statue of Évangeline.',
  'A memorial park with the church, the Évangeline statue, a Victorian garden and, in the ground, the traces of the Acadian village. The Register notes archaeological remains of Acadian occupation. The Parks Canada page says the garden and exterior grounds are open at all hours all year.',
  '',
  'Run by Parks Canada at 2205 Grand Pré Road. Grounds open 24 hours all year; the hours of the buildings were not read. Mapped lot on Grand Pre Road about 270 m NE; parking not checked.',
  [HP('Grand-Pré National Historic Site of Canada',3435),L('Parks Canada: dates and hours','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/ns/grandpre/visit/heures-hours'),W('Grand-Pré National Historic Site')],
  brick='?',hours='Gardens and exterior grounds open at all hours all year; buildings: not checked'),
N('covenanters-church-grand-pre','Covenanters\' Church','Grand-Pré','ns','worship','standing',45.09974,-64.30486,'exact','view','drive','','1804–1811',
  'A frame church dated between 1804 and 1811, built as a Presbyterian meeting house without a tower and later called the Covenanters\' church when the congregation renewed the covenant of its forebears. A square tower and steeple were added to a gable end. It is a national historic site for being a typical 18th-century New England meeting house, five bays wide with the door on the long side.',
  'A white wooden church on a hill above Grand-Pré, in a treed churchyard walled with a low stone wall. The Register lists, for the interior, a high three-tiered pulpit with an octagonal sounding board and moulded panelling, a three-sided stepped gallery and box pews. Whether the public can go in was not checked.',
  '',
  'At 1989 Grand-Pré Road, in the Grand-Pré rural historic district. Look from the road and churchyard; opening times not checked.',
  [HP('Covenanters\' Church National Historic Site of Canada',12343)],
  brick='no'),
N('balmoral-grist-mill','Balmoral Grist Mill','Balmoral Mills','ns','industry','standing',45.64559,-63.19508,'site','open','drive','','',
  'A wood-frame water-powered grist mill on Matheson Brook, restored and run as part of the Nova Scotia Museum. The Register calls it one of the last of a once-common link between farm and kitchen, with a kiln, grinding and bagging floors laid out in the order of milling. A dam and an iron turbine once drove it.',
  'The mill and its kiln, the old stable used as the reception, a wooden footbridge over the dam, and walking trails in the woods. The mill still grinds some products.',
  '',
  'Nova Scotia Museum site at 660 Matheson Brook Road. A path leads to the mill from the parking lot. Hours were not read, so none are given here.',
  [HP('Balmoral Grist Mill',15146)],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('sutherland-steam-mill-denmark','Sutherland Steam Mill','Denmark','ns','industry','standing',45.70944,-63.15986,'exact','view','drive','','1890s',
  'A steam-driven woodworking mill of the 1890s that stood beside the Intercolonial Railway\'s Short Line in the middle of a large woodland. It became a museum in 1970 and joined the Nova Scotia Museum in 1975. Wikipedia reports that in 2026 the Nova Scotia government announced it would be closed permanently.',
  'The mill building and its machinery stand beside the highway. As of the closing announcement it is shut to visitors; check before going.',
  '',
  'At 3169 Highway 326. Closed as a museum according to Wikipedia; the government\'s own notice was not read. Look from the road.',
  [W('Sutherland Steam Mill Museum')],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('chignecto-ship-railway-fort-lawrence','Chignecto Ship Railway: Fort Lawrence Terminus','Fort Lawrence','ns','rail','ruins',45.8401,-64.267,'site','view','drive','canal','1888–1891',
  'Henry Ketchum\'s ship railway was meant to lift ships out of the water at the Bay of Fundy, put them on a cradle of 192 wheels and haul them 17 miles across the Isthmus of Chignecto to the Northumberland Strait. Work stopped in 1891 with about three quarters done: the basin at Fort Lawrence was finished and some 20 km of track laid, but the cradles and locomotives never ran. The stone work and masonry left at the terminus are still there.',
  'Remains of the dock walls and masonry at the Bay of Fundy end. The Register says much of the stone and rail has been taken away. At the Tidnish end there is a stone-arch bridge that carries a 4 km walking trail, the Henry Ketchum Trail.',
  'A railway built to carry ocean ships overland.',
  'The Register says the property at the Fort Lawrence Terminus and most of the track bed is private; look from public roads and ask before going on. The Tidnish end is a provincial picnic park.',
  [HP('Fort Lawrence Terminus',7832),W('Chignecto Marine Transport Railway')],
  brick='?'),
N('beaubassin-fort-lawrence','Beaubassin and Fort Lawrence','Amherst','ns','town','traces',45.8478,-64.2618,'area','view','drive','forts','1672–1755',
  'Jacques Bourgeois and other settlers from Port-Royal founded the Acadian village of Beaubassin in 1672. In 1750 the French burned it as a British army under Major Charles Lawrence advanced, and the British then built Fort Lawrence, three wooden barracks and two blockhouses, on the east side of the Missaguash River. The French answered by building Fort Beauséjour opposite; Fort Lawrence was abandoned after Beauséjour fell in 1755. Parks Canada bought much of the Beaubassin land in 2004 and the site was designated in 2005.',
  'Nothing stands. The Register says the earthworks, visible until 1991, may survive under a dairy barn and yard, with archaeological remains across the property Parks Canada administers. A stone marker for the village stands near the Nova Scotia visitor centre off the Trans-Canada Highway in Amherst.',
  '',
  'Working farm and Parks Canada land at Fort Lawrence Road; no visitor facilities found. Look from the road. The pin is on the Register\'s point for Fort Lawrence, an area, not a structure.',
  [HP('Fort Lawrence National Historic Site of Canada',13271),W('Beaubassin')],
  brick='no'),
N('argyle-courthouse-tusket','Argyle Township Court House and Gaol','Tusket','ns','civic','standing',43.85486,-65.97445,'exact','open','drive','','',
  'A two-storey Greek Revival court house on the Tusket River, the seat of justice and government for the District of Argyle for 175 years. Its length was doubled in 1833 and it was extended again in the 1870s, which is when the courtroom took its present look. It is now a museum and a national historic site, designated in 2005.',
  'The Register lists the original jury benches, the prisoners\' docket and the judge\'s podium, the windowless cells and iron door on the main floor, a curving wooden stair to the gallery, and a wooden cupola.',
  '',
  'At 8168 Highway 3. A mapped lot is beside the building on Highway 3. Hours not read.',
  [HP('Argyle Township Court House and Jail National Historic Site of Canada',15162)],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('chebogue-meeting-house','Chebogue Meeting House','Rockville','ns','worship','standing',43.77871,-66.10144,'exact','view','drive','','1860',
  'An Italianate church built in 1860 near where the first New England Planter families settled in Yarmouth Township in 1761. Before a first meeting house went up in 1766 near the river, those families travelled on foot, horseback or boat. The Register ties it to the Rev. Jonathan Scott.',
  'A wooden church with a centred projecting tower, round-headed paired windows, drip moulds and a granite foundation, beside the Chebogue Cemetery. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At 7 Town Point Road, Rockville. Look from the road; hours and access to the inside not checked.',
  [HP('Chebogue Meeting House',5170)],
  brick='no'),
N('cape-forchu-lightstation','Cape Forchu Lightstation','Cape Forchu','ns','light','standing',43.79412,-66.15531,'exact','view','drive','','1839–',
  'The first light here was lit on January 15, 1840 to mark the entrance to Yarmouth harbour. The present station is a double keeper\'s house of 1912, a concrete fog alarm building and an "apple core" tower of 1962, the prototype of that design, and in Canada it was the first working lighthouse transferred to another level of government. Its Fresnel lens has been in the Yarmouth County Museum since 2000.',
  'The red and white apple-core tower at the end of the Cape Forchu road, with the 1912 house and the fog alarm building, on rock above the Atlantic.',
  '',
  'At the end of Highway 304; parking is on the road beside the station (mapped lots on Leif Ericson Trail and Highway 304). Hours of the building not checked.',
  [HP('Cape Forchu Lightstation',5205),W('Cape Forchu')],
  brick='no'),
N('mcculloch-house-pictou','McCulloch House','Pictou','ns','landmark','standing',45.67743,-62.72294,'exact','view','walk','','c.1806–',
  'Built about 1806 as a one-and-a-half-storey brick house by the Rev. Thomas McCulloch, who arrived in Pictou from Scotland in 1803, opened a school on this lot and went on to found Pictou Academy and to be the first president of Dalhousie University. The Register calls it one of the oldest buildings in Pictou County and among the oldest brick buildings in Nova Scotia.',
  'A three-storey brick house in lowland Scottish style with sandstone accents and a gambrel roof, on a gently sloping lot. The colour of the brick and the inside were not checked.',
  '',
  'At 100 Haliburton Road, a municipal heritage property; look from the road. Whether it is open was not checked.',
  [HP('McCulloch House',1273)],
  brick='?'),
]
